WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.920
We had a beautiful display at the market each
week.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.840
Pretty soon we had a lot more than we could
eat.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.080
And we said 'gosh we got to do something with
this.'

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.200
So we really thought we had it made.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:19.320
Anita and Jim McHaney are retirees who moved
from Houston to the Texas countryside in 2013.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.440
Their plan was to live well and grow food
on a 10 acre homestead

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.420
earning extra money selling produce at the
local farmer's market.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.580
We did amazingly well in the early stages.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:35.440
They grew beets, okra, carrots, kale and Swiss
chard.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.740
Soil out there is very sandy and the beets
just grew like mad.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:43.740
Now the obvious thing to do, when you got
more beets than you can sell

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.160
is to make pickled beets and can them.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.640
Like most states, however, Texas has a so-called
Cottage Food Law

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.180
that exempts certain items sold at farmer's
markets

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.820
from the state's commercial food manufacturing
regulations.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:04.540
Foods like bread, produce, nuts, jams, popcorn
and, of course, pickles.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.540
But, what constitutes a pickle?

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.080
And who gets to decide?

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:14.720
The McHaney's discovered that the Texas department
of state health services takes the narrow view.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.560
What are pickles?

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:22.560
Pickles are made from cucumbers that have
been preserved in vinegar, brine or a similar solution.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.700
Only pickled cucumbers are allowed under the
Cottage Food Law.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.320
Legislators didn't say that, the health department
did.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.700
All other pickled vegetables are prohibited.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.460
So, in order to sell their pickled beets at
the farmer's market

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:41.000
the McHaneys needed a commercial food manufacturer's license,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.560
to build a commercial kitchen,

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.999
to submit the recipes to a government contractor at Texas A&amp;M university

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.140
and to register for a $700 food manufacturing
class.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:54.600
And it's only once a year, so if you miss
it this October

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.600
then it's a whole year before you get another shot at it.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.560
We got right down to signing up for the class,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.700
I was gonna take it despite people said 'you
won't learn a damn thing in there.'

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:15.520
And then saw that $700 dollars and said 'you
know, this is crazy, this is insanity.'

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:17.780
Preposterous.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:23.080
The McHaney's neighbor Virginia Cox, also
grows and sells food at the local farmer's market.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.940
She'd like to be able to pickle her leftover
okra.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.840
I could make a whole lot more on it pickled

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.860
because if it doesn't sell this week, I can
take it back to the market next week.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:38.920
If it's fresh and it doesn't sell this week,
it's not gonna sell next week.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.160
Today the McHaney's farm lies fallow.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.080
But they have decided to take the state of
Texas to court

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:51.820
with the help of the Dallas law firm Drinker,
Biddle and Reath who took on the case pro bono.

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:56.120
Their argument is that the regulations on
cottage food production are unreasonable

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.600
and stifle their economic opportunities.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.780
People can't afford that kind of stuff, that
shuts them out.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:09.000
As precedent, the McHaney's case invokes the
landmark 20015 decision by justice Don Willett

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.420
striking down occupational licensing for Texas
eyebrow threaders.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:18.040
Their challenging the Health Department's
definition of pickles and subsequent regulation

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:23.920
on the grounds that it impinges on their constitutionally
protected right to earn an honest living.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.720
The Texas Department of State Health Services
wouldn't comment on the pending lawsuit.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:34.640
But defended the agency's rule as reflecting
the most common interpretation of the word 'pickle.'

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.471
It forces you if you want to do a pickled
beet

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:46.820
to go to a 700 dollar class that actually
nothing to do with home canning.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:50.780
And now we've got hundreds and hundreds of
occupations

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:54.160
that you gotta pay the government to do your job.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.820
For now the case is still pending and it's
just entered the discovery phase.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.900
But the McHaneys are optimistic about it's
outcome.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:07.160
You know we talk about pickling beets, but
it's a lot lot bigger issue.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:15.080
It's about economic freedom and government
by bureaucrat regulations that got nothing,

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.920
well it's who bought and payed for the bureaucrat.

